00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:07.160
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.590
So the name of this plan
is called Casimir's trick.

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:11.360
But what we're
actually going to do

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.160
is we're going to
evaluate or learn

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:15.560
how to deal with
spin information

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:19.210
in the calculation of
our matrix elements.

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:22.010
So now what is it
we're trying to do?

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:24.010
So the first problem
we might have

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:26.140
is that we have
polarized particles.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:28.690
So we have here again our
example of electron muon

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:29.530
scattering.

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:33.310
And if you assume that
the electron and the muons

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:36.370
are polarized, you will
find as we discussed

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:39.670
in the previous lecture
that our matrix element is

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:46.090
proportional to the adjoined
vector of mu 1 times

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.850
some sort of gamma
matrix times the spinor.

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:51.580
And we probably
have a polarization

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:53.710
that is as well
included here to give

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:59.730
the polarization of the photon
involved at the propagating.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:00.610
Good.

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:02.970
Now in order to now
get a number for M,

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:06.870
we actually have to be explicit
about the base function

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:08.160
of the external particles.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.530
And you can do this-- you
can just write this down.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:13.740
However, in experiments,
we are often

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:17.970
interested in the scattering
of unpolarized particles.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:21.270
Even if you have a way
to polarize a beam,

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.465
your polarization is
not going to be perfect.

00:01:23.465 --> 00:01:24.840
So in your
calculation, you might

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:30.890
want to average or
some opposite available

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:32.780
spins of your particle.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:35.640
So how are we going to do this?

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:39.170
So we're trying to calculate
the spin average amplitude.

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:40.625
Why averaging?

00:01:40.625 --> 00:01:43.040
We want to average
over the polarization

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.180
of the incoming
particles, again,

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:48.760
because we don't know what
the polarization states are.

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:51.740
And we want to sum of the
polarization of all final state

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:54.830
particles simply because
each polarization

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:56.960
state is a possible
outcome of the interaction.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:00.258
And we have to sum over
all possible outcomes.

00:02:00.258 --> 00:02:01.560
Good.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:06.020
So how do we calculate the
spin average amplitude?

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:08.919
So again, we start
from our matrix element

00:02:08.919 --> 00:02:10.810
where we have the
adjoined vectors and gamma

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:14.920
matrix or spinor.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:18.410
If you then calculate the
square of this matrix,

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:19.870
we find this solution here.

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:20.740
Great.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.230
And now we're just
doing a few tricks.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:26.860
So first of all, we can
write our adjoined mu 1 equal

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:31.490
to mu [INAUDIBLE] 1, gamma 0.

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:35.150
And then just continue
this rewriting to refine

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:36.900
this part here of
he solution, which

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.530
looks a little bit
simpler where we

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:44.870
now the adjoined matrix instead
of the adjoined matrix given

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:45.690
here.

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:52.500
So we want to evaluate what this
adjoined u1 gamma u2 is times

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.770
adjoined u2 gamma adjoined u1.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:57.270
Good.

00:02:57.270 --> 00:03:02.580
So this was just matrix algebra
and working with gamma so far.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:06.430
Now we're using this
completion relation

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:08.890
where I probably
haven't told you yet

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:13.150
what this syntax here is.

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:19.240
So gamma flash is
equal to gamma mu p mu.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.680
And that's just the way to
simplify it writing down

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:22.880
the equation.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:26.500
So if you use this
so-called Feynman's slash,

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:31.835
you can also rewrite that Dirac
equation in the simple form phi

00:03:31.835 --> 00:03:37.280
del flesh minus M y equal to 0.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:39.320
That's now our
direct [INAUDIBLE]..

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:45.980
Remember there was
a gamma mu in here.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:48.410
All right, let's just
decide now again.

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:52.490
We have to sum over all
spin states, or the spin

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:55.650
states of all those particles.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:58.490
Now if you start with
something over particle 2,

00:03:58.490 --> 00:03:59.640
we can rewrite this here.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:04.040
We use this equation, this
completely-filled relation,

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:07.232
and just really find this q
as equal to this part here.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.250
So now we have
this equation here,

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:14.820
which looks a little bit
simpler, because we just

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:21.760
have our incoming and outgoing
spinors for particle 1 given

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:23.230
here.

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:26.530
All right, now we're looking at
this part of the equation now.

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:30.260
And again, we're just doing a
little bit of matrix algebra

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:30.760
here.

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:37.510
And you find that this is
equal to q u 1 mu 1, i-i,

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:40.960
which is just simply something
over the same indices, which

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:44.640
is the same as building
the trace of this matrix.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:47.730
All right, so if you just
put all of us together,

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:51.980
you find that calculating
the sum of matrix elements,

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:55.430
it's similar to building
the trait of a matrix.

00:04:55.430 --> 00:05:00.490
And then our final results
then give you [INAUDIBLE]..

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:05.590
All right, so summarizing this
part, we have a matrix element.

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:07.900
And we saw that the matrix
elements of this form's

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:09.520
proportional to this form.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.430
And then when we try to
calculate the spin average

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:15.700
matrix element squared,
that's equal to the traits

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:18.940
of the particles involved
using the completion image.

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:22.420
There's an additional
factor of 1/2

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:24.460
here, which comes
from the averaging

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:26.110
over the initial spins.

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:30.250
So assuming exactly one
of u1 and u2 corresponds

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:34.410
to the initial particles
defined as vector 1/2,

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:38.530
if both initial
particles are the same,

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:42.730
like in a parent relation,
then vector is 1/4.

00:05:42.730 --> 00:05:44.665
And if neither is in
the initial state,

00:05:44.665 --> 00:05:49.640
then the factor is 1, which you
would have for fair production.

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:54.040
Good.

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:57.280
So now we simplified the
calculation of our matrix

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:00.400
elements, putting the
specific spin states

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:05.800
in there and the specific
polarization vectors to what

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:09.320
the calculation of
traces of matrices.

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:11.300
So now we can look
at what does it mean.

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:15.140
So this is what Casimir's
trick really is about.

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:20.790
So summing over spins reduces
to summing over matrices.

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:25.080
If you have antiparticles,
the completeness relation,

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:31.710
which uses two p/minus M,
and then you we go ahead.

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:34.750
So all you need to know now
is how to do this trace it.

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:37.590
So first, some general
remarks on traces.

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:39.960
We have to matches,
A and B. You want

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:43.260
to calculate the
trace of A plus B. Set

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:46.950
equal to the trace of
A plus the trace of B.

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:50.250
If you have a multiplicator
here, a vector alpha,

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:57.550
you can just take this out of
the calculation of the trace.

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:01.900
In the traces, two
matrices commutate.

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:05.643
The trace of A times B is equal
to the trace of B times A.

00:07:05.643 --> 00:07:07.060
And then you can
use this in order

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:09.246
to show a more
complicated relationships.

00:07:14.390 --> 00:07:17.140
Good.

00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:20.350
We have already started playing
around with the gamma Here

00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:22.780
are a few more identities
which might be of use when

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:25.990
you calculate
traces and calculate

00:07:25.990 --> 00:07:27.430
matrix elements overall.

00:07:27.430 --> 00:07:33.760
The first one is g mu nu
times g mu nu is equal to 4.

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:37.240
The anti-commutator relations
we already discussed

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:40.150
and have shown in one
of our recitation.

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:41.650
And if you have
three matrices you

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:46.120
can rewrite them
as minus 2 times

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:49.960
the matrix, which is
in the middle here.

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:52.160
So I'm not going through
this in much detail here,

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:54.140
but I encourage
you to just follow,

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:56.890
it's an additional exercise.

00:07:56.890 --> 00:08:01.610
We didn't have the opportunity
yet to play with the quantum.

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:05.030
And then you can use
those tricks, for example.

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:10.450
And commutator relation
to calculate the traces.

00:08:10.450 --> 00:08:15.970
For example, the gamma
mu, is 4 times g mu nu.

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:19.580
OK, so what you see
here is basically

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:23.600
used this anti-commutator.

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:26.870
Put it in here,
use this part here,

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:29.630
this is basically one
trade-off, 1 is 4.

00:08:29.630 --> 00:08:33.470
So we get g mu-mu
times the trace the 1.

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:36.210
But 4 times 4 matrix is 4.

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:38.929
And so you get the trace
of gamma mu gamma mu

00:08:38.929 --> 00:08:44.280
is equal to 4 times
g mu, and so on.

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:48.000
Later on, we haven't
discussed gamma 5 so much,

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:51.980
gamma 5 is defined as 5 times
gamma 0, gamma 1, gamma 2,

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:52.700
gamma 3.

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:55.560
We will see them and discuss
the weak interaction.

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:58.030
That there's a prominent
role will come up

00:08:58.030 --> 00:09:00.810
this very special gamma matrix.

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:04.560
And here are just some
pieces of information

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:06.290
for traces for gamma 5.

00:09:06.290 --> 00:09:08.620
The trace of gamma 5 is at 0.

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:10.410
The trace of gamma 5 times--

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:15.570
sorry-- a different
gamma matrix is zero.

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:18.805
And that's also true for the
product of additional gamma

00:09:18.805 --> 00:09:22.470
matrix that you can
find when you just

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:25.862
try to calculate this relation
and the relation of that.

00:09:28.790 --> 00:09:32.060
One last piece of the
traces, this gamma

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:35.150
matrices, it's shown here.

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:37.880
Only with four or
more gamma matrices

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:40.430
can you define or
find a non-zero trace

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:41.780
involved in gamma matrices.

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:44.960
And here's one
example where you have

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:48.740
the product of five or four
gamma matrices with gamma 5.

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:50.370
And that's equal in this case--

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:53.840
4 times 3i times the
total asymmetric tensor.

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:57.110
The total asymmetric tensor
is defined as minus 1,

00:09:57.110 --> 00:10:01.250
or even for rotation of
those numbers here plus 1

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:04.400
for odd permutations
and if there's

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:06.650
two instances of this state.

00:10:06.650 --> 00:10:08.930
All right, again,
I'm not showing you

